    "stem": "Zubin tests whether $49$ is a perfect square by subtracting consecutive odd numbers starting from $1$: $49-1=48$, $48-3=45$, $45-5=40$, and so on.\n\nOne or more options may be correct. Which two claims are correct?",
    "item_type": "mcq",
    "options": {
      "A": "$49$ is a perfect square because the test is a sum of odd numbers.",
      "B": "Continuing $1$, $3$, $5$, $7$, $9$, $11$, $13$ in order reaches exactly $0$ after seven steps, so $49=7^{2}$.",
      "C": "The sum of the first seven odd numbers starting at $1$ equals $49$.",
      "D": "$49$ is a perfect square because its units digit is $9$."
    },
